Today I did a coding challenge at codewar.com.
These were the instructions:
Define a function that takes an integer argument and returns a logical value true or false depending on if the integer is a prime.

Per Wikipedia, a prime number ( or a prime ) is a natural number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself.

Requirements

You can assume you will be given an integer input.
You can not assume that the integer will be only positive. You may be given negative numbers as well ( or 0 ).
NOTE on performance: There are no fancy optimizations required, but still the most trivial solutions might time out. 
Numbers go up to 2^31 ( or similar, depending on language ). Looping all the way up to n, or n/2, will be too slow.

First I checked if the number even is a prime number. I decleared a varaible called limit, which gives me the square root of the number.
Then I used a loop to check if the number is dividable by another 
number other than 1 or the number itself. If true, the functions returns False. If no other divisors were found, 
the functions returns True.
